{"kind":"report","text":"R2 ruling 2: the backfill interface and the first slice (answers af0d09ae; refines 3fd5bec0 item 6).\n\n1. Interface: neither A nor B. No new admin entrypoint and no direct provider write. A scope object can verify its own applied genesis from its own storage: entry 0 and the seed digest equal to its ID. So registration comes from the target itself:\n   a. Mode setting, default enforce. In `register` mode, a lookup of an unregistered ID still reaches the target, as today, and a target with a verified applied genesis registers itself in the fixed index on that access. A target without one registers nothing.\n   b. Sweep. The planner lists the scope IDs that the room's record names: the register, the directory and its application and lane items, membership, rules, destination and inbox, and each created child. The dry run prints each ID with its genesis hash, from ordinary authenticated reads. The sweep reads each listed scope once, so each registers.\n   c. Count check: the index count equals the listed count; any difference is listed by ID.\n   d. Enforce: the planner sets the mode to enforce and redeploys.\n   An old schema-only object is in no record, so the sweep never reads it and it never registers. Caller-supplied bytes never prove anything. The credential boundary is the planner's existing one: the Cloudflare account for the mode setting and redeploy, and the operator member key for the authenticated reads. Give the dry run and sweep as one documented command (a script or a dap subcommand), and list it in the runbook.\n   If the builder finds a scope that the record does not name but that must stay reachable, report it before building a different interface.\n2. First slice scope: the narrow slice is accepted. Gate unknown lookups (reads, proofs, site, applications, registry) through the index. Creation after R1's outer admission may initialize the target as today. Only an applied genesis registers. A creation that R1 admits and the native judge then refuses keeps its target storage and does not register, so later lookups refuse it. Name that residual in the head report: it is bounded by R1 admission and the R12 creation windows. Zero target SQL for refused creations is not required.\n3. Unchanged: native judgment, genesis commit before registration, exact retries and full-reference checks, and the legitimate lifecycle.","body":{"to":"builder"}}